Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Company Material Adverse Effect" means any significant deviation, event, or development that (a) has a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, or overall ability of the Company and its Subsidiaries to perform the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, or (b) would delay, prevent, or impair the consummation of the Merger. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following exceptions shall apply, and any effect solely resulting from these factors shall not be considered a Material Adverse Effect:
   (i) General macroeconomic fluctuations, including volatility in foreign exchange rates and shifts in global interest rates; 
   (ii) Broad industry trends that do not cause the Company’s performance to deviate by more than a 50% incremental adverse impact compared to its peers; 
   (iii) Acts of God such as earthquakes, hurricanes, pandemics, or government-imposed restrictions—except where such events uniquely disrupt the Company’s ability to obtain regulatory approvals or materially delay the consummation process; 
   (iv) Regulatory or legislative changes that apply uniformly to the industry, provided these are not accompanied by special measures targeting the Company; and 
   (v) The public announcement of this Agreement, unless accompanied by a documented exodus of key executives critical to consummating the transactions.
For all purposes, however, the 'ability to consummate' clause shall not fall within these carveout exceptions if the adverse effect directly impairs or delays the procurement of essential third-party consents required to close the Merger. (Pages 10-11)
